  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "single-color rectangular foreground objects on a background; exactly one object encloses a 4-connected background hole",
  "transform": "replace the grid with the color of the object whose bbox contains an enclosed background component",
  "grid_shape": "1x1 label cell"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 14-16 rows x 13-17 cols, background 0
  objects:
    count: 4-7 single-color 4-connected rectangular foreground objects
    colors: each object has a distinct non-zero color (from 1-9)
    bbox_dims: 2-8 rows x 2-8 cols per object
    shape: all objects are axis-aligned filled rectangles except the frame
    separation: bboxes do not overlap; min gap >= 1 cell between any two bboxes
    touching_border: 0-4 objects may touch the grid edge [observed]
  frame:
    exactly_one: one object is a rectangular frame enclosing a bg-colored hole
    border: the frame's bbox boundary is entirely filled with the frame's color
    hole: a rectangular connected bg region inside, not touching the bbox border
    hole_dims: 1-2 rows x 1-3 cols
    thickness: >= 1 on each side; may be non-uniform (1-3 per side)
  fg_coverage: 33-57% of grid cells are non-background [observed]
  output: 1x1 grid containing the frame object's color

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction
  steps:
    1. Pick grid dimensions (14-16 x 13-17) and number of objects N (4-7).
    2. Choose N distinct non-zero colors; designate one as the frame color.
    3. For the frame, pick bbox dims (>=3 on each axis), pick per-side thickness
       (>=1, ensuring hole dims >= 1x1), fill border ring with frame color.
    4. For remaining N-1 objects, pick bbox dims (2-8 each axis), fill solidly.
    5. Place all N bboxes on the grid with no overlap and min gap 1, using
       greedy or random placement with rejection on collision.
    6. Verify exactly one object has an enclosed bg hole (guaranteed by construction).
```
